==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|deuzan|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|dheusom|lang=ine-pro}}.

Germanic cognates include Old Saxon ''dior'' (Dutch ''[[dier]]''), Old High German ''[[tior]]'' (German ''[[Tier]]''), Old Norse ''[[dýr]]'' (Danish {{term|dyr}}, Swedish ''[[djur]]''), Gothic [[𐌳𐌹𐌿𐍃]] ''dius''.  The Indo-European root also led to Lithuanian ''dvēsti'', Lettish ''dvēsele'', Slavic ''*duša'' (Russian ''[[душа]]'').

